# Keep the working tree on LF, whatever platform it was checked out on.
#
# Asset busting hashes the bytes of a file as they lie in the working tree, so
# a CRLF checkout and an LF checkout give the same asset two different names -
# and a name is not something the test suite can normalise the way it
# normalises the line endings inside a file. Git's Windows default converts to
# CRLF on checkout, which would make every published asset name depend on who
# built the site. This overrides that, and states for Git what .editorconfig
# already states for editors.
#
# Git detects the fonts and images as binary on its own and leaves them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

layouts/partials/version.txt merge=ours
